EXT. RUINED CHURCH TOWER - NORMANDY - DAY (1944)

A shattered bell tower, half the roof torn open to the sky. Pigeons nest in broken beams. Stone dust sifts on a floor strewn with prayer books. Beyond the shell of the nave, a dim valley of poplars and burned barns.

LT. KAMAU NJOROGE, 30s, a Kenyan serving with the British, kneels at an arrow slit with binoculars, his helmet pushed back, a streak of soot across his forehead. Beside him, CPL. DUVALL, 20s, a Louisiana man with a drawl and a mouth full of chewing tobacco, cradles a Browning automatic rifle. In the corner, PVT. SANTOS, 18, shivers under a blanket, a field radio on his lap, headset askew.

KAMAU
Quiet on the ground.

DUVALL
Ground's always quiet till it isn't.

SANTOS
Sir, Captain Reyes on the net.

Kamau crosses and takes the headset. Santos hands it over with both hands, like a sacrament.

KAMAU
Tower Six, Lieutenant Njoroge.

REYES (V.O.)
(through static)
Njoroge. Status?

KAMAU
Two hundred meters. No movement. Barn on the left is empty. I'd say they've pulled back.

REYES (V.O.)
I'd say they're resting. Hold your position. We push at fourteen hundred.

KAMAU
Understood.

REYES (V.O.)
Njoroge. Make sure the boy eats.

KAMAU
Yes, sir.

He lowers the headset. Turns to Santos, who is pale and trying not to look it.

KAMAU (CONT'D)
Eat.

SANTOS
I'm not hungry.

KAMAU
Private, that wasn't a request.

Santos unwraps a ration bar with trembling fingers. Duvall spits tobacco over the ledge, and the three of them listen to the valley breathe. Somewhere below, wind rattles through the splintered pews.

DUVALL
Sir, you ever think about what happens after?

KAMAU
After what?

DUVALL
After this.

KAMAU
I think about my mother's farm.

DUVALL
Mine's got a pecan orchard.

SANTOS
I just want a hot bath.

Duvall chuckles. Kamau almost smiles.

Then, far away, a single muffled THUMP. Another. Mortar shells.

All three men freeze. Dust trickles from the beams. Santos covers his head. Duvall rolls toward the slit, raising the BAR.

KAMAU
(low)
Hold.

DUVALL
Sir?

KAMAU
Not at us. They're shelling the road.

They wait. The thumps continue, rhythmic, distant. The tower trembles. No shell comes near.

At last Duvall lets his breath out.

DUVALL
Hell of a thing, waiting.

KAMAU
Yes.

Santos slowly lowers his hands from his head. Looks up.

SANTOS
Is that a hot bath coming?

KAMAU
(smiling, finally)
Possibly. In the next war.
